  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 AM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nWeak shortwave ridging aloft across eastern SD/NE will shift east \nthis morning as an upper low over western WY slowly meanders east \ninto parts of western SD and NE by the end of the day. Low stratus \nand some patchy fog will be possible in some areas through the \nmorning. Increasing chances for steady showers will make their way \ninto central SD later this morning into the afternoon as that upper \nlow slowly shifts east. This rain is progged to slowly move east as \nfar as the James Valley and parts of the Big Sioux Valley tonight \ninto early Monday. This system will slowly move east along the SD/NE \nborder tonight through Monday. The rain will gradually taper off \nfrom north to south on Monday. Rainfall amounts are expected to be \nhighest across parts of central and south central SD where between a \nhalf inch and three-quarters of an inch will be possible today \nthrough the end of the event on Monday. Temperatures will remain \nbelow normal the next couple days as the low stratus persists \nunderneath this disturbance.\n\nWeak ridging aloft will attempt to work into the region on Tuesday \nbut gets shunted more east into the western Great Lakes. \nNevertheless, the overcast skies should break giving way to more \nsunshine and drier conditions. Temperatures will begin to modify \ncloser to normal. Even warmer conditions are expected mid to late \nweek as southwest flow aloft re-establishes itself across the \nNorthern Plains. With another passage of a shortwave trough through \nthe region Wednesday into Thursday the next round of showers and \nthunderstorms are expected. By the end of the period, upper ridging \nis expected which will help return drier conditions to the area \nalong with temperatures at or slightly above normal late in the week.",
